From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 12:43:17 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: survive Metal command queue timeouts

Metal reports MTL4CommandQueueErrorTimeout when one submission exceeds the
time the system allows, which is not the same as the GPU failing or the
device breaking. An instrumented ES31-for-GL shard measured the two
timed-out submissions of its run executing for 0.177804 s and 0.438908 s
while a submission that succeeded in the same run executed for 0.452050 s,
longer than either, and both cases that carried a timeout still verified
their own rendered output as a pass. How long the GPU ran does not predict
the report.

The commit callback answered every command queue error with permanent
device loss. One report therefore destroyed a queue that kept working,
failed the Venus context and aborted the whole guest CTS process after 115
of 3906 cases. Two campaign shards died worse: the timeout was followed
immediately by a texture descriptor whose width and height were both zero,
and Metal answers that with an assertion inside
-[MTLTextureDescriptorInternal validateWithDevice:] that ends the host
process.

Classify the failure from the execution interval Metal reports alongside
it. A timeout Metal accounted for from start to finish leaves a live queue
behind, so it is reported and survived rather than thrown away along with
every result that would have followed it; a shard carrying six such reports
resolved all 3906 of its cases. What the report puts in doubt is that one
submission's contents, which this driver cannot repair and Vulkan cannot
express short of device loss, so the report says exactly that instead of
claiming the results are good.

What separates a live queue from a stopped one is whether Metal admitted
the submission, not whether it measured execution for it. A Vulkan CTS
fraction on an A15 reported three timeouts in a row: the first two carried
an execution interval and were survived without disturbing the run, and the
third reported an equal start and end. That third report is the queue
killing work it had already taken, which is what it does to whatever is
queued behind a submission that has just timed out. Reading it as a hang
lost a queue that was running, wedged the guest and ended the fraction
97,646 cases early. So a timeout for a submission carrying a start
timestamp is survived, and only one the queue never admitted, or started
and abandoned without ever timestamping an end, still loses the device.

Every other error stays device loss too, carrying the error name, the
execution interval and the raw host GPU clock so it stays visible and two
processes that fail at once line up against each other. One classifier
decides both the disposition and the wording, and Metal's own text goes
last, so a long message cannot truncate away what happened.

The report also carries the reason the system gives, which Metal nests
under the error. A GPU hang in any process makes the system discard the
in-flight work of every process, and Metal reports each discard with the
same timeout code and an execution interval of tens of milliseconds, so
without the reason a bystander reads as the culprit. On an M1 Ultra a
render loop's submissions failed with "Discarded (victim of GPU
error/recovery) (00000005:kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorInnocentVictim)"
while another process's own report read "Caused GPU Hang Error
(00000003:kIOGPUCommandBufferCallbackErrorHang)", and each of the six
timeouts that glmark2, Android and CTS guests whose own work does not hang
reported on a shared host in one day was such a discard.

Refuse an image extent Metal cannot represent before it reaches Metal.
Vulkan already requires every texture descriptor size field to be at least
one, so a conforming caller sees no difference, but this driver serves a
guest across a protocol and has to answer an out-of-contract image with a
Vulkan error instead of with the host process. A chroma plane divides the
image extent, so the check belongs per plane, and the two entry points that
cannot report an error now answer with no memory requirement and an empty
subresource layout.

Make the bridge's mtl_command_queue_error_to_string name every command
queue error as well, including the deprecated device removal code and
codes this driver predates, instead of leaving an unmodeled value without
a name on the error path. The report and its test use that one function.

The diagnostics topic that owns the message predates the KosmicKrisp test
scaffolding by fourteen topics, so this contract and its regression tests
land as their own topic rather than perturbing an unrelated topic's patch.

An end timestamp is only a measurement while it is credible. Metal puts a
fixed placeholder in the end timestamp of a submission it has no end for at
all: every genuine hang an A15 has reported carries
gpu_end_s=6148826548.220315, and the report claimed the GPU had accounted
for six billion seconds of execution. That placeholder is the one thing in
a report that separates a submission whose own work never finished from one
the queue killed on entry, so an interval longer than a minute -- already
far past anything a stopped workload can have measured -- is not an
interval, and such a report says the GPU started the submission and never
reported it finishing. The disposition does not change: Metal admitted the
submission, so the timeout is still survived.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
